+{"body":"In the streaming branch of `DetokenizerManager._decode_batch_token_id_output`, across consecutive incomplete-UTF-8 (`�`) recovery steps, `sent_offset` is set to `decoded_text_len + len(find_printable_text(new_text))`. `find_printable_text` (`utils.py:377`) is non-monotonic: its penultimate-CJK branch (`:388`) emits through a trailing CJK char, while the later last-space branch (`:392`) retreats to an earlier space, so `len(printable)` can shrink from one recovery step to the next. A shrinking `sent_offset` makes the next clean step's `pending` too small, and it re-emits already-streamed text.\n\nExample `new_text` deltas over three streaming steps: `\"A 世�\"`, `\"A 世a�\"`, `\"A 世ab\"`. Step 1 emits `\"A 世\"` (`sent_offset=3`); step 2 retreats to `sent_offset=2` (`find_printable_text(\"A 世a�\") == \"A \"`); step 3 re-emits from offset 2, so the client stream is `\"A 世世ab\"` instead of `\"A 世ab\"` — the CJK char is duplicated. This is realistic CJK-plus-byte-BPE streaming traffic. Non-streaming requests are unaffected (they materialize once from the committed text).\n\nFix (clamp `sent_offset` so it never retreats within a recovery run): #31564.\n\ncc @hnyls2002 @hari-hm","comments":[],"state":"OPEN","title":"Streaming detokenization can duplicate a character (sent_offset retreats across recovery steps)","url":"https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/issues/31598"}
